# What each colour space family is, what a colour in it is made of, and
# where this interpreter and the specification differ.
#
# PLRM 4.8 defines eleven colour space families. Three name a device's
# own colorants, four are built on the CIE 1931 XYZ space, one indexes a
# palette, two carry tints through a transform into another space, and
# one paints with a pattern rather than a colour.
#
# This is the largest family here, and the one where a member has twice
# been reached by whichever code its author thought of. The component
# count was written twice and the second copy knew the indexed and tint
# families and not the CIE ones. The cross product that should have
# caught that listed six bases and no CIE family. The characterisation
# test listed nine members of eleven, so the two pre-extensions were
# asked nothing. Each of those was one list not held to another.
#
# Membership is DERIVED from the roster in data/color.ps, which
# tests/check-colour-spaces.sh already holds against the two pricing
# tables. So a family added there and given no line here fails, and a
# line here for a family the roster drops fails too.
#
#   <family>  <kind>  <components>  <paints|mute>  <reason>
#
# components  how many operands setcolor takes, MEASURED from the count
#             the space is entered holding
# paints      a region filled in this space puts ink on the page,
#             COUNTED. Acceptance is not the question: a space that is
#             set without complaint and marks nothing is what a
#             half-wired conversion looks like

DeviceGray    device   1  paints  the device's own grey, one component
DeviceRGB     device   3  paints  the device's own red, green and blue
DeviceCMYK    device   4  paints  the device's own cyan, magenta, yellow and black
CIEBasedA     cie      1  paints  one component through a decode and a matrix to CIE XYZ, PLRM 4.8.3
CIEBasedABC   cie      3  paints  three components, the family the other CIE spaces reduce to
CIEBasedDEF   cie      3  paints  three components pre-extended through a three-dimensional lookup table, PLRM 4.8.3
CIEBasedDEFG  cie      4  paints  four components pre-extended through a four-dimensional lookup table, whose entries are one array level deeper than a DEF table's
Indexed       indexed  1  paints  one index into a palette, resolved in the base space
Separation    tint     1  paints  one tint through a transform into an alternative space
DeviceN       tint     2  paints  one tint per named colorant, so the count is the length of the names array rather than a property of the family
Pattern       pattern  1  paints  a pattern rather than a colour, and the one component is the pattern itself

families 11

# The lookup-table widths and shapes a CIE pre-extension accepts.
#
# PLRM 4.8.3 gives a DEF space a Table of [ NH NI NJ table ] where table
# is an array of NH strings of 3 x NI x NJ bytes, and a DEFG space a
# Table of [ NH NI NJ NK table ] where table is an array of NH arrays,
# each of NI strings of 3 x NJ x NK bytes. The extra array level is the
# whole difference between the two families, and it is the thing a reader
# written for one and used for the other gets wrong.
#
# PROBED, one space per case.
#
#   table <family> <case> <takes|refuses>

table CIEBasedDEF   well-formed   takes
table CIEBasedDEF   short-strings refuses
table CIEBasedDEF   short-array   refuses
table CIEBasedDEFG  well-formed   takes
table CIEBasedDEFG  flat-array    refuses
table CIEBasedDEFG  short-strings refuses

tables 6

# What each special colour space's own parameters must be, and what each
# family answers when they are not.
#
# The three special families other than Pattern each carry parameters
# nothing else does: an Indexed space a palette and the index range it
# holds, a Separation the colorant it names, a DeviceN the array of
# colorants it names. Every one of those is stated by PLRM 4.8.4 or
# 4.8.5, and every one of them is quiet when it is wrong -- a palette too
# short reads colours off the end of its string, a lookup that is a
# number leaves that number where the base space wanted its components,
# a bare colorant name where an array belongs prices the space by the
# length of the name.
#
# So they are PROBED here, one space per case, and the answer recorded is
# the error name rather than the fact of a refusal: which error a program
# is given is what tells it whether it handed over the wrong kind of
# thing or a thing of the right kind with a value outside what the family
# admits. PLRM's setcolorspace entry names typecheck for the first and
# rangecheck for the second.
#
# BOTH directions: a well-formed space of each family is probed here too,
# since a check that refuses what the specification admits is worse than
# the one it replaced.
#
#   param <family> <case> <takes|errorname>

param Indexed     well-formed        takes
param Indexed     hival-too-big      rangecheck
param Indexed     hival-not-integer  typecheck
param Indexed     lookup-short       rangecheck
param Indexed     lookup-number      typecheck
param Separation  name-string        takes
param Separation  name-number        typecheck
param DeviceN     names-string       takes
param DeviceN     names-not-array    typecheck
param DeviceN     names-number       typecheck
param DeviceN     names-empty        rangecheck

params 11

# An uncoloured pattern paints its cells in the space underneath it, so
# every family above is also a base a pattern can be laid over. That
# crossing is where this family's two live defects were found, one at a
# time, and both times the list that should have caught them was a list
# of bases somebody wrote out.
#
# So it is PROBED here as a product: an uncoloured pattern over each
# base, ink counted. Pattern itself is not a base -- PLRM 4.9.1 gives a
# pattern space an optional underlying space and a pattern is not one.
#
#   base <family>  <paints|mute>

base DeviceGray    paints
base DeviceRGB     paints
base DeviceCMYK    paints
base CIEBasedA     paints
base CIEBasedABC   paints
base CIEBasedDEF   paints
base CIEBasedDEFG  paints
base Indexed       paints
base Separation    paints
base DeviceN       paints

bases 10

# Where this interpreter and the specification differ, and what is to
# become of each difference. A difference the guard can find and this
# list does not name is a failure; so is a line here naming a difference
# the guard can no longer find.
#
#   <what>  <settled|thorn|heading>  <reason>

missing-required  settled  A space whose family requires a parameter and does not carry it raises undefined when it is set, because the parameter is fetched and the fetch is what fails. The same is true of a halftone, a shading, a form and a function, so it is one behaviour across five families rather than this one being odd, and it would be changed in all five or none. It is recorded in each of their registers

space-array-rebuilt  settled  currentcolorspace does not hand back the object the program supplied. A family named by a bare name comes back as a one-element array, and a one-element array comes back as a different one-element array; the multi-element forms come back as the same object. PLRM 8.2 requires currentcolorspace to answer an array describing the current space and does not make it the program's array, so a program cannot rely on identity either way. What it can rely on is that what comes back can be handed straight back to setcolorspace, which the family test asserts for every member

special-names-in-devicen  settled  PLRM 4.8.5 says the special colorant names All and None are not allowed in a DeviceN names array, and one there is accepted and painted through the transform like any other name. Accepting it is the common reading, so refusing it would turn pages that render everywhere today into errors for a rule nothing enforces. The names are meaningful only to a Separation, where they are honoured

cie-whitepoint-unconstrained  settled  PLRM 4.8.3 requires a CIE-based space's WhitePoint to have a Y component of 1.0 and X and Z components greater than zero, and BlackPoint components no less than zero, but states no error for a dictionary that says otherwise, and setcolorspace's own entry names none either. A WhitePoint whose Y is 2.0 is taken; an X or Z of zero is taken as well and surfaces later as undefinedresult out of the arithmetic that divides by it, which reports against whatever painting operator reached the conversion rather than against the operator that supplied the dictionary. Refusing either would be choosing an error the specification does not name, so both are left as they are and recorded here

divergences 4
